{
  "A": "Develop the primary O(n^2 log H), O(n)-space replacement using polynomial reduction. Over modulus p=1000000007, define Q(x)=x^n-P1*x^(n-1)-...-Pn. Compute r(x)=x^(H-1) mod Q and return sum_i r_i*A_(i+1). Use left-to-right binary exponentiation: polynomial squaring followed, for set bits, by an O(n) multiply-by-x/reduction rather than another general multiplication. Reduce high coefficients in descending degree; preserve the stated coefficient order. Handle n=1 correctly, where x mod Q=P1. Start with straightforward exact modular arithmetic, then investigate symmetric squaring and scratch-buffer reuse. Eliminate all dense matrices and fixed-size matrix copies. Provide the indexing argument and measured whole-program results, not merely operation-count estimates.",
  "B": "Develop an independent generating-function/Bostan-Mori solver. Set S(x)=sum_i A_(i+1)*x^i, Q(x)=1-P1*x-...-Pn*x^n, and R=(S*Q) mod x^n. Extract coefficient H-1 of R/Q by repeatedly multiplying by Q(-x), retaining numerator coefficients matching the exponent's parity and even denominator coefficients, then halving the exponent. Begin with parity-aware schoolbook convolution; prioritize computing only coefficients actually needed. If the fixed budget permits, compare an exact NTT convolution backend for larger n. Modulus 1000000007 does not support the required radix-2 transforms: use suitable auxiliary primes and CRT, with their product exceeding (n+1)*(p-1)^2. Reconstruct and reduce every convolution modulo p before the next iteration, not just the final answer. Length 2048 suffices for full products at n=1020. Measure rather than assume the transform crossover; parallel auxiliary-prime work is optional only under the unchanged build and four-core limit.",
  "C": "Target short-horizon and low-latency execution. Implement direct recurrence evaluation with a rolling n-element state, costing O(n*(H-n)) work and O(n) space; include exact scalar modular powering for n=1. Supply this direct solver as an integration-ready component. For an independent general fallback and small-n control, replace the baseline identity accumulator and dense base matrix with the state vector [A_n,...,A_1]: apply companion-matrix powers to that vector on set bits of H-n, and square only when another exponent bit remains. Use input-sized storage and separate square-output buffers, avoiding the fixed 1022-by-1022 objects and returned matrix copy. Investigate 32-bit residues with widened products and safe batched reduction; eight ordinary products plus a reduced carry fit in uint64_t, whereas an unrestricted n-term sum does not. Prioritize allocation and arithmetic savings over extensive cubic-kernel tuning. Report evidence for dimension-based direct-iteration crossovers.",
  "G": "Integrate only programs actually produced and validated by A/B/C. Preserve each strongest standalone candidate before combining changes. First compare A and B as complete general solvers, then evaluate adding C's direct short-horizon path and useful storage/arithmetic changes separately. Select dispatch thresholds from measured costs using n and H-n, never test identities or expected outputs. Keep serial execution for workloads where threading does not amortize its overhead; use at most four CPU workers through facilities supported by the fixed build. Check one-based indexing, coefficient orientation, n=1, H=n+1, zero and maximal coefficients, modular subtraction, accumulator bounds, and coverage of every valid input. Use the unchanged evaluator and existing budgets; do not alter tests, oracle, compiler, target or permissions. Judge actual end-to-end hybrid timings against standalone candidates and baseline, accounting for variability. Integration gains are not additive: retain a standalone winner whenever a hybrid regresses.",
  "analysis": "Only src/npbonacci.cpp and Problem C are relevant. The baseline is correct on four reported cases, averaging 0.019804 seconds; no input dimensions, profiling, or optimized measurements are supplied. Its companion-matrix exponentiation costs O(n^3 log(H-n)), performs dense work although only one answer is needed, and uses large fixed arrays with potential copying overhead. The recurrence admits much smaller polynomial algorithms. Assign A the quadratic polynomial method, B an independent coefficient-extraction method, and C short-horizon/low-overhead execution. The target is four-core ARM64 without GPUs. At the observed roughly 20 ms runtime, startup and allocation may matter, but their dominance is not established. All improvements must preserve correctness for n<=1020 and H<=10^9, not merely the four observed cases."
}